## Proof

**Answer: Yes**, $(S_k - \mu N_k)/(\sigma\sqrt{N_k})$ converges in distribution to the standard normal, provided $\sigma > 0$.

**Setup and reduction.** Interpret $S_k = \sum_{i=1}^{N_k} X_i$ (the sum of the first $N_k$ terms of the sequence). Set $Y_i := (X_i - \mu)/\sigma$, so that $\{Y_i\}$ are i.i.d. with $\mathbb{E}[Y_i]=0$, $\mathbb{V}[Y_i]=\mathbb{E}[Y_i^2]=1$, and finite second moment. Then
$$\frac{S_k - \mu N_k}{\sigma\sqrt{N_k}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{N_k}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_k} Y_i =: T_k.$$
It suffices to show $T_k \xrightarrow{d} \mathcal{N}(0,1)$.

**Characteristic function via conditioning.** Let $\phi_Y(t) = \mathbb{E}[e^{itY_1}]$ be the characteristic function of $Y_1$. Since each $N_k$ is independent of the entire sequence $(X_1, X_2, \dots)$, hence of $(Y_1, Y_2, \dots)$, conditioning on $N_k = n$ gives
$$\mathbb{E}\!\left[\exp\!\left(it\,\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\sum_{i=1}^{n} Y_i\right)\right] = \left(\phi_Y\!\left(\tfrac{t}{\sqrt{n}}\right)\right)^n,$$
using the independence and identical distribution of the $Y_i$. Therefore
$$\phi_{T_k}(t) = \mathbb{E}\bigl[e^{itT_k}\bigr] = \mathbb{E}\!\left[\left(\phi_Y\!\left(\tfrac{t}{\sqrt{N_k}}\right)\right)^{N_k}\right]. \tag{1}$$

**Local expansion.** Since $\mathbb{E}[Y_1]=0$ and $\mathbb{E}[Y_1^2]=1<\infty$, the characteristic function admits the expansion
$$\phi_Y(s) = 1 + is\,\mathbb{E}[Y_1] - \tfrac{s^2}{2}\,\mathbb{E}[Y_1^2] + o(s^2) = 1 - \tfrac{s^2}{2} + o(s^2), \qquad s\to 0.$$
Substituting $s = t/\sqrt{n}$ with $n\to\infty$:
$$\phi_Y\!\left(\tfrac{t}{\sqrt{n}}\right) = 1 - \tfrac{t^2}{2n} + o\!\left(\tfrac{1}{n}\right),$$
and consequently, by the standard exponential limit,
$$\left(\phi_Y\!\left(\tfrac{t}{\sqrt{n}}\right)\right)^n \xrightarrow{n\to\infty} e^{-t^2/2}. \tag{2}$$

**Almost sure pointwise convergence of the integrand.** By hypothesis $N_k \to \infty$ a.s., so for almost every $\omega$, $N_k(\omega)\to\infty$. Applying (2) with $n = N_k(\omega)$:
$$\left(\phi_Y\!\left(\tfrac{t}{\sqrt{N_k(\omega)}}\right)\right)^{N_k(\omega)} \xrightarrow{k\to\infty} e^{-t^2/2}, \qquad \text{a.s.} \tag{3}$$

**Dominated convergence.** Since $|\phi_Y(s)|\le 1$ for all $s$ (being a characteristic function), we have the uniform bound
$$\left|\left(\phi_Y\!\left(\tfrac{t}{\sqrt{N_k}}\right)\right)^{N_k}\right| \le 1,$$
which is integrable. Applying dominated convergence to (1) and using (3):
$$\phi_{T_k}(t) = \mathbb{E}\!\left[\left(\phi_Y\!\left(\tfrac{t}{\sqrt{N_k}}\right)\right)^{N_k}\right] \xrightarrow{k\to\infty} \mathbb{E}\!\left[e^{-t^2/2}\right] = e^{-t^2/2}.$$

**Conclusion.** The characteristic function of $T_k$ converges pointwise to $e^{-t^2/2}$, the characteristic function of the standard normal. By Lévy's continuity theorem,
$$\frac{S_k - \mu N_k}{\sigma\sqrt{N_k}} = T_k \xrightarrow{d} \mathcal{N}(0,1).$$

The two ingredients that make this work are: (i) the independence of each $N_k$ from the entire sequence $(X_i)$, which allows the conditional characteristic function to factor as a pure power; and (ii) the almost sure divergence $N_k\to\infty$, which combined with the boundedness of characteristic functions permits the exchange of limit and expectation via dominated convergence. $\blacksquare$

